A||||22||Ferrari_cdn||Fieros problematic?||||5||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||07-12-2001||09:24 PM||||My brother in law's uncle has a '84 Fiero, just rebuilt with an new engine.  I don't know much about it, I just asked my brother in law to ask his uncle questions like what engine it is and how new, etcetera.  I've started looking at different Fiero sites, and it makes me worry about if Fieros are always breaking down and requires to be repaired frequent, or are they not?  (If they do, how often do they break down, in average)  Or you guys just like to tune them up?  I'm not very knowledgeable on fixing cars and all, but my brother in law has a friend who fixes Fieros.  I just want to know more about Fieros, and thanks for your feedback.<P>EDIT:  How are Fieros for long distance driving, like driving somewhere 5 hours away, and how does it fare in winter?  Where I live (Winnipeg, Canada) the winters can get very bitter sometimes and we get a lot of snow.  (But our summers can be very hot too,    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 07-12-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||mbramble||07-12-2001||09:30 PM||||I have an 87 Coupe 4 cyl that I would get in and drive across country at any time.  I do regular maintenance (oil/filter changes, plugs, etc.).  It has been running for the two years that I have had it with no real problems.  It has been in my family since '94 (two of my kids had it previously).<P>You just have to remember that the last Fiero was built in 88 so they are all at least 13 years old.  You have to expect problems with any 13+ year old car.  How much problem will mostly depend on how it has been maintained through it's life.  They are fun cars to drive and relatively cheap to buy so many of them have been beat up pretty badly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||07-12-2001||09:31 PM||||Welcome to the Forum.  You have done two thing right so far. You have found someone who fixes Fieros and you found this place.  There are alot of very knowledgable people here and posting questions usually get results. Without a doubt you will learn about cars while owning a Fiero which is cool too.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||07-12-2001||09:59 PM||||The 1984 Fiero is 17 years old.  Tell me which 17 year old car is not problematic?  <P>So whether you have a 1984 Mustang, Taurus, Chevrolet, BMW or Cadillac, it has broken down, requires repairs and has a rusted body.<P>If you find a car that you enjoy, you repair it.  After 17 years for an 84 or 13 years for an 88 Fiero, with any sort of routine driving the car will have over 100,000 miles on it.  The car will need repairs.  That is a fact of mechanics.  Cars wear out.  If you are going to worry about a car breaking down, you do not by a 13-17 year old car.  You buy a 2 year old Honda.<P>I have an 87 GT, which has never broken down since I bought it in 1987.  I maintain it and repair, replace things before they break.  I also have an 88 GT (bought used) which has been less than reliable.  Only two major breakdowns where I was stranded in the four years I've owned it. That is what AAA is for. It has been expensive to repair.  New engine, transmission, suspension, steering rack, painting and on and on.  I now have a new 88 GT. The work was farmed out to people who know how to fix them.<P>Last Saturday I drove 750 miles in 12 1/2 hours in my 87.  I drove to Florida and back in it this spring, 2930 miles round trip.  It drive very nicely on the highway at 75 mph. I've been from Maine to Wisconsin in my 88 this year. Its fun to drive on twisty roads, but that gets tiring after a few hours.  My experience that the GT drove like crap in the snow.  Too much torque with the V6 and a 5 speed transmission and fat tires. Low to the ground, so you have a plastic snow plow. My Fieros are stored in the winter.<P>If you are old and fat like me, sliding around on my back under a jacked up Fiero in my driveway is not a fun afternoon.  Find a good mechanic who has the tools to do the job and dig deep into your pockets.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Ferrari_cdn||07-12-2001||10:17 PM||||I know that it is old and it can be problematic, I just meant that if Fieros are "more" problematic than other brand of cars, that was all I was looking for.<P>My sister told me that "it was rebuilt with a new engine", so I'm not sure if she meant a brand new engine or just rebuilt it with new parts.  I will know by tomorrow, and if it was with a new engine so I won't have to worry about breakdowns as often as if it had a 17 year old engine under its hood.  But thanks guys, and thanks for the welcome!  I'll be sure to visit here often.<P>I don't think I like white cars, and thats the colour of the car he has.  If I am going to repaint it, what colour do you guys recommend?  I've always liked blue, but I don't know if it fits Fieros.||||reg||7
Z||000005||hyhy4u||07-12-2001||10:19 PM||||A fiero is defidently one of those cars that if you take care of it (regular oil changes and stuff), it will take care of you..if you dont take care of it....well you better have a mighty big pocket book cause you will have a ton of expensive problems.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||07-12-2001||11:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<BR><B>...I've always liked blue, but I don't know if it fits Fieros...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe you already know this, but for the  1987 model year, Pontiac produced some blue Fieros.  I think they look great, and the color fits the car just fine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>||||reg||6
Z||000007||new fiero||07-13-2001||12:15 AM||||I had a 88 coupe for 3 years (before I killed the body) and Had no major problems at all.  Drove from Colorado Springs, CO. to Biloxi,MS. twice and enjoyed the trips both ways!!  Drove it daily and nightly!  Now have moved the 88 engine into a new 86 body and the engine is still kickin like it used to....  MAN I MISSED MY FIERO....Needs a lot of cosmetic work (mostly on the inside) but it was worth the $200 I paid for the body.<P>------------------<BR>newfiero<BR>(swapping 88 L4 to 86 and having fun!)<BR>Yahoo! Messenger id: newfiero||||reg||6
Z||000008||ka4nkf||07-13-2001||01:00 AM||||I have had lots of cars in my time, but the fiero is the best car I have ever owned. The fiero will last as long as any car if it is taken care of. Mine is a 86 SE V6 Auto with 74,000 and it runs and drives as good as the day it was new. They are not a race car and if you drive it right and service it right it will last you a long time. (remember it is not a good family car, but sure makes a good comuter).||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ferrari_cdn||07-13-2001||01:19 AM||||Ok sounds great.  I was just told that this car I'm getting has 56,000 kilometres.  Thats very low so it sounds good.  He had it for 5 years and no major problems at all.<P>One thing I've also wondered, where would I put a few of my stuff if I'm going to my cottage?  What is under the front hood, anyway?  I've seen a picture where we can put some cargo in the back, but not much space, anyone know the dimensions for a '84?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||07-13-2001||01:40 AM||||Cool! Another 'Pegger!<BR>Welcome in! My experience so far has taught me a couple of things:<BR>1) If you do repairs yourself, the Fiero is one of the sweetest deals you can drive. Replacement parts for 84-87 (at least on the v6) are CHEAP. Sometimes they need to be ordered in, but CHEAP!<BR>2)This car is more solid than my previous Econo-box (mazda) or my brother's Toyota.<BR>3)It will break down, like any other 14-17 year old car. Does it do it more than any other car? No reason it should, as long as it's taken care of. We own a Jaguar, and an Alfa Romeo. Don't talk to me about reliability  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>4) This place rocks. I've had more help here than ANY of the parts shops, mechanics, or dealers here in town.<BR>5) I don't ever regret buying this car.<P>Your brother-in-law's friend doesn't live on Henderson, just past the perimeter, does he? I might know him  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000011||artherd||07-13-2001||04:43 AM||||Hi there! Welcome to the fourm, it's a great place with plenty of helpful souls all sharing :)<P>If you treat your Fiero like a Ferrari, it will behave like one.<P>That is to say, give it the regular matinence (fluids, brakes, grease all fitings, keep engine bay clean, don't dump the clutch at 6,000 five times a day, etc.) then it will reward you with one of the most enjoyable driving expiences around!<P>Like any older used car, all bets are now off.  You might get a cherry car that has been well cared for, and driven only weekends and has very few miles.  Or you may get a car that has been flogged to death and has an engine that is about to die, and a dry suspension that needs total replacement.<P>If you want a reliable car, something to get from point a to point b, which drives well in the snow, and gets great gas mileage.  Do not buy a fiero.<P>If you want a car that is FUN TO DRIVE, handles like a dream, needs regular matinence and care, is something truely special, and makes *getting there* half (or more) the fun, then by all means, join the rest of us nuts!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000012||Old Lar||07-13-2001||07:50 AM||||Don't plan on packing much under the hood.  That is where the spare tire resides, the radiator, power brakes & A/C stuff.  For the trunk area, it is deep, but not wide.  Duffle bags work great because they form to the shape.  The trunk, it was designed to hold two full size golf bags.  Don't put anything unprotected that needs refrigeration in the trunk, cause it gets warm from the muffler located just in front, inside the engine compartment.||||reg||10
Z||000013||new fiero||07-13-2001||08:56 AM||||I must dissagree with artherd when he said:<BR>If you want a reliable car, something to get from point a to point b, which drives well in the snow, and gets great gas mileage. Do not buy a fiero.<P>My 88 coupe(Iron Duke 2.5) did all of that easily.  Throw some chains on the back wheels and a sandbag in front and it is great fun in the snow.  Also, I was getting about 32 MPG on the highway.  <P>------------------<BR>newfiero<BR>(swapping 88 L4 to 86 and having fun!)<BR>Yahoo! Messenger id: newfiero||||reg||1
Z||000014||Galen||07-13-2001||09:23 AM||||I'll have to take new fiero's side on this, However little bit of snow we do get own here in southern NC, I drive my fier yearround(when I have somewhere to go). When we got around 17 inches a year or so ago I drove it, Handled rather well seeing as how I'd never driven in 'icey' or 'snowy' conditions before. And had less than 6 months of driving total under my belt. Right now I would not take my 88 coupe too far out of town for fear of my tranny going further south on me. For some reason they dont like to be empty of tranny fluid, why that is I do not know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">Anyways, I'll finish my rant up saying, As soon as I fix my tranny(no biggie <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) I would not hesitate to take the car anywhere my pocket could afford to take it. With gas prices down to 1.29 around here.. sweeet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/redcreature.gif">   <BR><FONT SIZE="1">To whom it may concern, The preceeding message was the opinion of this person and not of this forum. If you do not like it. Oh well</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000015||k2m4||07-13-2001||09:36 AM||||Let me tell you i live in upstate new york and have owned a fiero for the last 15 years.  My 2M4 has been and still is the best winter car ive ever driven( without chains too). I have over 154,000 on the car right now but drives better than most cars with 30,000 on it. i do all the maintenence on it and its not that expensive just VERY TIME CONSUMING!!!!! when you do the repairs.  Its a daily driver driving the city  jammed streets of albany and the northway with no heat problems. Of course i am a fiero die hard so my feelings on them are that they are just ritous.!!<BR>My suggestion is take the car from your sister or whom ever and drive it for the day. Bring your sunglasses with you and have a little fun with it and youll see  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Welcome BTW im new here too and i must say i wish i found this place along time ago!<P>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||07-13-2001||10:22 AM||||go up to the search function the top of the page and search on the Subject line:<P> how reliable is your Fiero<P>  there are two threads in the archives with the same subject line, about a year apart<P>  I posted my opinions in the newer one near the end. <P>  I think we tend to suffer from the 'Love is Blind' syndrome on this forum - Fieros do have a rep as being more troublesome than other cars - cracked exhaust manifolds, headlights that wont lift up, fuel pumps that are a major task to replace, head gaskets, suspension bushings<P>   I think you will find in general that most Fieros that are on the road now have had significant mods made to them to replace the original parts that were 'weak' from the factory - so if you get a car that has been gone over from bumper to bumper it could be a sweet little reliable machine<P>   If you get one that is completely stock, like my '85SE is, well lets just say I have a list of things that needs to be done to my car, and it seems like when I get one thing checked off the list I find two more that I add.<P>  make sure you understand what is being talked about by the different replies - you can drive a fiero around with a cracked exhaust manifold - its a little louder and the milage will be about 20% lower than it should be - but it will still run - so when you are talking about reliability do you simply mean, will it run? or do you mean: will it also stop on a dime - will the headliner tear if I take out the sunroof - will the car leave little oil puddles everywhere you park it...?<P>   but like someone else said, if you are gonna own a 15 year old car you should expect to work on it or pay someone else to - thats why you can buy one for $1500 when a comparible new car (like a honda DelSol or a Miata) will cost you ten times that or more.<P>  BTW - I find Fieros are very comforable on long rides - lots of room in there - sit in the passenger seat and push it all the way back - But you better take a good look at the trunk behind the motor - thats all you get - not much room for hauling camping supplies.<P>  I have heard people complain Fieros are squirrly in the snow, but I have owned at least 10 aircooled VWs and like the way a rear engine rear wheel drive car handles in the snow, so I expect mine to be the same - and it should have plenty of heat - and the electronic fuel enjection should fire up just as well at -10° - so if you understand how a rear engine car handles when it spins out - whether you consider that 'good in the snow' is up to you.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000017||Mach10||07-14-2001||12:17 PM||||Ken: You drive long distances from the PASSENGER SEAT?!?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>How do you reach the pedals!?!||||reg||11
Z||000018||Ferrari_cdn||07-14-2001||07:23 PM||||LOL!||||reg||10
Z||000019||artherd||07-15-2001||03:15 AM||||Well, my 2.5L iron puke sure wans't reliable. I think it threw at least TWO rods.<P>I am on my third fiero, this time it's the second 2.8 I've had in a row developing rod knock. in less than 5kmiles of driving.<P>Sigh.<P>They're *FANTASTIC* cars, but the engines are **** . Plain and simple.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000020||Ken Wittlief||07-15-2001||09:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Ken: You drive long distances from the PASSENGER SEAT?!?!?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>How do you reach the pedals!?!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>you must have the sun roof out to do this:  set the cruise control to 75mph, push the drivers seat all the way back, and climb over to the other seat - steer with your left hand.<P>  passing drivers assume its a british car.<P> [on long trips there are usually two people in the car - so you will spend half your time in the NAV seat]<P>||||reg||11
Z||000021||ginoferrari||07-15-2001||03:58 PM||||Another Ferrari and a new member too. I have an 84 2M4 that I've had since 86. I've done just about all the work on it myself. I haven't had to do to much to it in all this time. 2 TPI's a CV joint boot and a head at 102k miles. I have 148k miles on it now and plan on using it for autocross. I also have a couple wrecked Fieros I've picked up as parts cars. One is an 84 SE and the other is a 87 coupe. I think the Fiero is the most comfortable long distance car I've ever driven. I've got a bad back and it doesn't bother it at all. I took a 760 mile trip to California that I did in 750 minutes including fuel and food stops. That's a 60 mph average which isn't bad, and it got about 33 mpg. I use it as a daily driver and think it's great in the snow after I removed the front air dam and put snow tires on it. I'm thinking about putting the TECH 4 engine out of the 87 in it but don't know if it's an easy swap or not. It still runs great and I don't plan on making the swap unless I really need to. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||07-15-2001||09:13 PM||||Ive put 86,000 miles on mine in less than 4 years. only one breakdown ignition module went out...1 minute fine next totally dead. Once other time OEM coil went but I did get it nursed home from 30 miles away. Ive driven to Dallas tx from ohio and back in one weekend with no problem, just stopping for BR and gas. It is comfortable to me too. I had some chest and stomach pains that no one could fix, muscles and arthritis. The only time I got any relief was driving my car, seemed like it was the perfect position. I couldnt sit on a couch for more than a couple hours. I still think I ought to get an old Fiero seat reupolstered for my living room   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. After a long while of medication problems now bearable.<P>Also ask Fiero Lisa. she must have put 10,000 miles on her GT since school let out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> back n forth across the county 1/2 dozen times.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
